    "excerpt": "A brand-new 2026 single-story home in Maricopa's Rancho Mirage community: 2,668 sq ft, 4 bedrooms, a 3-car garage and a FlexSuite with its own private garage bay. Listed at $454,990 with quartz counters, an oversized island and a four-panel sliding glass door to the backyard.",
    "metaDescription": "Tour 37715 W Nina Street in Maricopa, AZ: a new-build 4-bed, 2,668 sq ft Rancho Mirage home with a FlexSuite and 3-car garage, listed at $454,990.",
    "content": "<h2>New Construction in Rancho Mirage</h2>\n<p>37715 W Nina Street is a new single-story home in Maricopa's <strong>Rancho Mirage</strong> community, built in 2026 and listed at <strong>$454,990</strong>. It is the Amethyst floor plan: <strong>2,668 square feet</strong>, <strong>4 bedrooms</strong>, a <strong>3-car garage</strong>, and a Modern Desert exterior. It is roomy, it is new, and at roughly $171 per square foot it is priced for buyers who want space without paying East Valley prices.</p>\n<p>The feature most buyers will notice first is the <strong>FlexSuite</strong>, a separate living area with its own private garage bay. A layout like this suits a lot of households: an aging parent, an adult child coming home, long-staying guests, a home office with its own entrance, a gym, or a studio.</p>\n<h2>Layout and Interior Features</h2>\n<p>The main living area is one open space. An <strong>oversized kitchen island</strong> sits at the center and looks out over the great room, so whoever is cooking stays part of the conversation. The finishes come from the builder's Roosevelt Collection and were chosen to be warm and durable rather than trendy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ash cabinetry</strong> with satin nickel hardware</li>\n<li><strong>Quartz countertops</strong> and brushed nickel fixtures</li>\n<li>An eat-in kitchen with a walk-in pantry and a large island</li>\n<li><strong>9-foot-plus flat ceilings</strong> throughout</li>\n<li>A <strong>four-panel center sliding glass door</strong> that opens the great room to the backyard</li>\n<li>A primary suite with a double vanity</li>\n<li>Natural gas heat, central air, and high-speed internet wiring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That big sliding door does a lot of the work. It brings in natural light and makes the patio feel like part of the house, which matters in a place where you can be outside most of the year. The rear yard is a blank slate, so buyers can add a pool, turf, a covered patio extension, or desert landscaping later.</p>\n<h2>Living in Maricopa</h2>\n<p>Maricopa has grown from a small farm town into one of the fastest-growing cities in Pinal County, and much of that growth comes from buyers priced out of Chandler, Gilbert, and Tempe. Rancho Mirage is near <strong>State Route 347</strong>, the main commuter road north toward I-10 and the Phoenix metro. Shopping, dining, and services are clustered along the 347 corridor.</p>\n<p>Some local highlights:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Copper Sky Regional Park</strong>, with a lake, sports fields, and a multigenerational recreation center</li>\n<li><strong>Ak-Chin Southern Dunes Golf Club</strong> and Harrah's Ak-Chin for dining and entertainment</li>\n<li>Public schools in the Maricopa Unified School District, plus several charter options</li>\n<li>Quick access to the Gila River Indian Community's Wild Horse Pass area for resorts, outlets, and entertainment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Daily life here is quieter and more suburban than in the core Valley. Commute times on the 347 have improved with recent widening and the planned overpass work, though buyers who drive into Phoenix every day should test the commute at rush hour.</p>\n<h2>Is It a Good Value?</h2>\n<p>In the most recent Maricopa new listings, most homes in the $300,000s are between about 1,600 and 2,300 square feet. At $454,990 this home costs more, but it adds roughly 400 to 1,000 square feet, a third garage bay, and a separate suite most resale homes don't have. Buying new also means current energy codes, builder warranties, and years before big-ticket replacements like the roof, water heater, or HVAC.</p>\n<p>New construction comes with its own homework. Buyers should read the builder's purchase contract closely, find out whether incentives such as rate buydowns or closing-cost credits are on the table, check HOA dues and CC&amp;Rs, and look into any community facilities district or special assessment on the tax bill. Builder contracts are written to protect the builder, so a buyer should have their own representation.</p>\n<h2>Attorney and Paralegal Oversight on Every Transaction</h2>\n<p>At <strong>Camelback Home Group</strong>, every transaction is reviewed by our in-house <strong>attorney and paralegal team</strong>. On a new-build purchase that review covers the builder agreement, the deposit and cancellation terms, warranty coverage, and any HOA or assessment disclosures, so you understand your rights before you sign.</p>\n<h2>Schedule a Showing</h2>\n<p>If you want a new home with room for multigenerational living or a home office without paying East Valley prices, 37715 W Nina Street is worth seeing. Call Camelback Home Group at <strong>(480) 203-3039</strong> to schedule a showing, ask about builder incentives, or get a custom comparison of nearby new-construction options in Maricopa.</p>"
